  "query_preview": "..\n\nKEY MESSAGE 1: Widespread rain will develop across the CWA \nSunday morning and bring light to moderate amounts of one-\nquarter to three quarters of an inch as the remnants of midwest \nTSRA/MCV moves east into the region. \n\nUpdate:\nWe are going to be (and in some cases have already begun)\ncollaborating with our neighboring offices on a Flash Flood\nWatch for tomorrow/Sunday. Confidence is high in a FF threat,\nbut narrowing down 1) where and 2) when the threat be highest \nand 3) when will it end are the major questions to answer.\n\nPrevious...\nAfter a warm front moves into Central and Northern PA early \nSunday afternoon, some breaks in the otherwise widespread,\nmulti-layered cloud cover will lead to MU CAPE values climbing\ninto the 1000-1500 J/KG range per the latest HREF. \n\nThunderstorms will develop near and to the south of this \nboundary bringing locally heavy rain in excess of one inch in \njust 20-30 minutes with the potential for localized flash \nflooding.\n\nERO from WPC hydro forecasters has nearly all of the CWA (nrn \ntier = not yet) in the SLGT risk category for Sunday.\n\nClouds will thicken up tonight with a several hour period of\nsteady light to moderate rain overspreading the state from west\neast during the early to mid morning hours Sunday, ahead of a \nwarm front. \n\nPWAT values will surge to 2.0+ inches across the part of the\nstate near and to the south of Interstate 80 by late Sunday\nmorning and stay there for the rest of the day. The warm front \nwill lift into Central PA Sunday afternoon, before being \nfollowed closely by scattered thunderstorms with periods of\ntorrential downpours in the afternoon and evening as a cold \nfront pushes through. \n\nBasin \"Average\" rainfall amounts Sunday will vary from around \none-half of an inch across the Northern Tier Counties of PA to \nbetween 1 and 2 inches across the Southern Half of the state. \nAny training thunderstorms could put down 3 inches of rain which\nwould trigger localized flash flooding of poor drainage areas \nand some streams/creeks.",
